WEBVTT

00:00:07.217 --> 00:00:08.597
Another drink sir?

00:00:08.597 --> 00:00:09.597
No thankyou.

00:00:09.597 --> 00:00:17.007
Excuse me - is anyone sitting here?

00:00:17.007 --> 00:00:19.087
No - please have a seat.

00:00:19.087 --> 00:00:22.677
That's better - my feet are killing me!

00:00:22.677 --> 00:00:23.677
Have you been here long?

00:00:23.677 --> 00:00:28.597
No, but I just flew in this morning, and I
haven't had a chance to sit down since then.

00:00:28.597 --> 00:00:31.687
Oh, where have you come from?

00:00:31.687 --> 00:00:33.787
From Manila.

00:00:33.787 --> 00:00:35.687
Is this your first visit to Australia?

00:00:35.687 --> 00:00:39.676
No, I have been once before, but it was a
long time ago.

00:00:39.676 --> 00:00:41.867
And have you been to Sydney before?

00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:45.577
No, it's an amazing city.

00:00:45.577 --> 00:00:50.517
Yes, it has its points. But you're lucky to
live in Manila. It's a fascinating city.

00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:54.437
What about yourself? Do you live in Sydney?

00:00:54.437 --> 00:00:57.467
No, I live in Melbourne. I'm just here for
the conference.

00:00:57.467 --> 00:01:02.007
I'm going to Melbourne later. What's the weather
like there?

00:01:02.007 --> 00:01:07.907
Not too good in winter, but at the moment
it should be okay.

00:01:07.907 --> 00:01:10.577
So, how's your hotel?

00:01:10.577 --> 00:01:14.717
It's good. Very convenient - just near the
harbour.

00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:16.287
Have you seen the Opera House yet?

00:01:16.287 --> 00:01:20.967
Yes, we flew right over it!

00:01:20.967 --> 00:01:25.876
Excuse me - there's someone I must talk to.
It's been very nice to meet you. I'm Walter

00:01:25.876 --> 00:01:26.876
by the way.

00:01:26.876 --> 00:01:36.287
You too. I'm Sue. Perhaps we'll meet later.

00:01:36.287 --> 00:01:40.916
I hope so.

00:01:40.916 --> 00:01:45.257
Today we're looking at a typical conversation
you might have with someone you've only just

00:01:45.257 --> 00:01:51.736
met - at a conference for example. What sort
of thing can you talk about - and what topics

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:57.596
should be avoided? Let's look at how Walter
and Sue get acquainted.

00:01:57.596 --> 00:02:04.237
Sue breaks the ice, or starts the conversation.
Excuse me, is anyone sitting here?

00:02:04.237 --> 00:02:10.327
No, please have a seat.
That's better - my feet are killing me!

00:02:10.327 --> 00:02:15.927
We can tell from how Sue speaks to Walter,
that they haven't met before. She is very

00:02:15.927 --> 00:02:23.817
polite, and so is he. But then she says something
more personal, and this is the icebreaker.

00:02:23.817 --> 00:02:30.377
That's better. My feet are killing me.
Sue is letting Walter know two things - firstly

00:02:30.377 --> 00:02:36.117
- that she is tired, and secondly that she
is willing to have a friendly conversation

00:02:36.117 --> 00:02:43.917
with him. By making a more personal, or casual
remark, she is inviting him to respond.

00:02:43.917 --> 00:02:45.487
Have you been here long?

00:02:45.487 --> 00:02:50.207
No, but I just flew in this morning, and I
haven't had a chance to sit down since then.

00:02:50.207 --> 00:02:54.347
Walter asks, "Have you been here long?"

00:02:54.347 --> 00:03:01.007
To start a conversation like this, it's fairly
safe to talk about what people have just done.

00:03:01.007 --> 00:03:07.977
For this, we use the present perfect -have.
Practise with Walter some typical questions

00:03:07.977 --> 00:03:14.677
like this you could ask.
Have you been here long?

00:03:14.677 --> 00:03:18.877
Have you been to Sydney before?

00:03:18.877 --> 00:03:22.947
Have you seen the Opera House?

00:03:22.947 --> 00:03:30.157
Have you tried any restaurants?
Questions that start with have you are yes/no

00:03:30.157 --> 00:03:36.017
questions, so they have a rising tone:
Have you been here long?

00:03:36.017 --> 00:03:42.247
When answering these questions in a situation
like this it is helpful to add some information,

00:03:42.247 --> 00:03:45.467
not just say yes or no.

00:03:45.467 --> 00:03:51.627
If you just say yes or no, people may think
you don't really want to talk.

00:03:51.627 --> 00:03:52.627
Have you been here long?

00:03:52.627 --> 00:03:55.357
No.

00:03:55.357 --> 00:04:01.157
Oh.
Instead - notice how Sue helps the conversation

00:04:01.157 --> 00:04:05.598
along by giving some extra information.
Have you been here long?

00:04:05.598 --> 00:04:11.147
No, but I just flew in this morning, and I
haven't had a chance to sit down since then.

00:04:11.147 --> 00:04:16.897
Oh, where have you come from?
Sue has said that she flew in this morning.

00:04:16.897 --> 00:04:19.987
So this gives Walter an obvious next question.

00:04:19.987 --> 00:04:23.987
Where have you come from.

00:04:23.987 --> 00:04:29.157
This is a different type of question - it's
asking for information.

00:04:29.157 --> 00:04:38.967
Questions beginning with where, when, what,
why, who are all questions asking for information.

00:04:38.967 --> 00:04:44.307
Notice the difference between, "Where have
you come from?" meaning where did you fly

00:04:44.307 --> 00:04:51.337
from, and "Where are you from?", meaning what
is your nationality.

00:04:51.337 --> 00:04:57.487
Notice also the falling tone with these questions:
'Where have you come from?'

00:04:57.487 --> 00:05:02.977
This makes the question sound friendly. But
be careful not to ask too many questions like

00:05:02.977 --> 00:05:09.467
this all together - the other person may think
you're being too nosy.

00:05:09.467 --> 00:05:10.467
Where are you from?

00:05:10.467 --> 00:05:11.467
Manila.

00:05:11.467 --> 00:05:13.107
What do you do?

00:05:13.107 --> 00:05:14.107
I'm an accountant.

00:05:14.107 --> 00:05:16.557
Why are you here?

00:05:16.557 --> 00:05:18.027
I'm on business.

00:05:18.027 --> 00:05:19.407
Who are you with?

00:05:19.407 --> 00:05:23.707
My boss. Excuse me.

00:05:23.707 --> 00:05:28.897
Where are you going?
Of course, some questions like this are alright

00:05:28.897 --> 00:05:35.756
but try not to sound too inquisitive and offer
some information or ideas yourself.

00:05:35.756 --> 00:05:37.647
Is this your first visit to Australia?

00:05:37.647 --> 00:05:41.717
No, I have been once before, but it was a
long time ago.

00:05:41.717 --> 00:05:43.677
And have you been to Sydney before?

00:05:43.677 --> 00:05:47.547
No, it's an amazing city.

00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:52.867
Yes, it has its points. But you're lucky to
live in Manila. It's a fascinating city.

00:05:52.867 --> 00:05:59.767
Sue doesn't just answer yes or no - she adds
some extra information. And Walter finds the

00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:07.047
opportunity to give his opinion, and to compliment
the place Sue comes from. Now it's Sue's turn

00:06:07.047 --> 00:06:12.157
to ask a question.
What about yourself? Do you live in Sydney?

00:06:12.157 --> 00:06:15.927
No, I live in Melbourne. I'm just here for
the conference.

00:06:15.927 --> 00:06:23.077
Sue wants to ask Walter about himself - this
is showing interest. So she says, "What about

00:06:23.077 --> 00:06:25.307
yourself?"

00:06:25.307 --> 00:06:29.017
Practise some useful phrases to introduce
a question.

00:06:29.017 --> 00:06:33.377
And what about yourself?

00:06:33.377 --> 00:06:40.097
And how about you?
These phrases should be followed by a question.

00:06:40.097 --> 00:06:47.897
Practise again, with the question to follow.
And what about yourself? Do you live in Sydney?

00:06:47.897 --> 00:06:58.566
And how about you? Have you been here before?
When meeting someone new on business, but

00:06:58.566 --> 00:07:05.727
in a social setting, there are a few safe
topics. We can talk about travel and accommodation,

00:07:05.727 --> 00:07:11.337
basic questions about the other person, about
the city you are in, interesting sights to

00:07:11.337 --> 00:07:17.587
see, and of course, the weather.
I'm going to Melbourne later. What's the weather

00:07:17.587 --> 00:07:18.587
like there?

00:07:18.587 --> 00:07:24.127
Not too good in winter, but at the moment
it should be okay.

00:07:24.127 --> 00:07:29.997
Finally, let's look at how Walter ends the
conversation. He needs to make sure the other

00:07:29.997 --> 00:07:36.927
person doesn't think he is bored.
Excuse me, there's someone I must talk to.

00:07:36.927 --> 00:07:39.697
It's been very nice to meet you.

00:07:39.697 --> 00:07:43.017
You too. Perhaps we'll meet later.

00:07:43.017 --> 00:07:47.577
I hope so.
He gives a reason why he must go, then says,

00:07:47.577 --> 00:07:54.267
"It's been very nice to meet you." Practise
some useful phrases for ending a conversation,

00:07:54.267 --> 00:08:00.197
with Walter and Sue.
Well, it's been very nice to meet you.

00:08:00.197 --> 00:08:03.957
Nice to meet you too.

00:08:03.957 --> 00:08:04.997
It's been good to meet you.

00:08:04.997 --> 00:08:09.827
You too.

00:08:09.827 --> 00:08:11.316
I have enjoyed talking to you.

00:08:11.316 --> 00:08:15.387
So have I.

00:08:15.387 --> 00:08:17.647
I hope we can meet again.

00:08:17.647 --> 00:08:22.477
So do I.

00:08:22.477 --> 00:08:25.346
Perhaps we'll meet again.

00:08:25.346 --> 00:08:31.357
I hope so.
In conversation, when asking questions remember

00:08:31.357 --> 00:08:38.177
to use a rising tone for yes/no questions
- such as those starting with 'do you' or

00:08:38.177 --> 00:08:40.385
'are you.'

00:08:40.385 --> 00:08:46.037
Questions starting with 'Do you' ask about
regular actions, and about likes and dislikes,

00:08:46.037 --> 00:08:47.797
or opinions:

00:08:47.797 --> 00:08:50.167
Do you travel often?

00:08:50.167 --> 00:08:52.676
Do you like the weather here?

00:08:52.676 --> 00:08:56.467
Do you think this session will be interesting?

00:08:56.467 --> 00:09:01.157
Questions starting with 'Are you' are asking
for personal information:

00:09:01.157 --> 00:09:04.357
Are you from Manila?
or intentions:

00:09:04.357 --> 00:09:10.777
Are you going to the dinner?
Questions starting with where, when, what,

00:09:10.777 --> 00:09:16.437
why or who are asking for information, and
they often have a falling tone:

00:09:16.437 --> 00:09:22.806
Where do you come from?
When are you going back?

00:09:22.806 --> 00:09:27.087
People from different cultures have different
ideas about what are reasonable topics for

00:09:27.087 --> 00:09:34.117
conversation between strangers so at first,
it is safest to stick to general topics such

00:09:34.117 --> 00:09:39.986
as travel, the weather, places, and of course
the business you are in.

00:09:39.986 --> 00:09:48.246
And remember, to keep the conversation going,
offer information, don't just ask questions.

00:09:48.246 --> 00:09:51.686
That's all today on the Business of English.
See you next time.